  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Scattered thunderstorms today, with lightning and gusty\n   outflow winds being the main threats. \n\n - A cold front arrives Saturday, bringing cooling temperatures, \n   gusty westerly winds, and precipitation, focused along and \n   south of about I-90. \n\n\nSatellite imagery early this morning indicates a circulation of \nlow pressure spinning off the coast of southern British Columbia. \nThis feature, along with a ridge of high pressure with its axis \njust east of the Continental Divide, will keep the Northern \nRockies under southwest flow aloft going into the weekend. Pacific\nmoisture from the southwest will bring chances for showers and \nthunderstorms again today. Model soundings show enough instability\nfor showers to start as early as this morning for Lemhi County \nand southwest Montana. Instability increases this afternoon, when\nthunderstorm activity will spread further north. High resolution \nmodels indicate chances for thunderstorms across north-central \nIdaho and northwest and west-central Montana. A dry lower \natmosphere will keep rain amounts on the low side, so primary \nimpacts will be gusty outflow winds (30-50 mph) and frequent \nlightning. Expect windy conditions this afternoon across much of \nthe Northern Rockies as the Pacific low moves inland and an upper \nlevel jet streak becomes situated over central Idaho and western \nMontana. Southern Idaho County, as well as Lemhi County and \nsouthwest Montana will be the breeziest, as gusts from the \nsouthwest reach about 30 mph this afternoon. \n\nAs the low traverses the Canadian border on Friday, it will push \na cold front across the Northern Rockies. Daytime high \ntemperatures will drop down to the upper 70s to low 80s for \nMontana valleys and low 90s for the lower valleys of western \nIdaho. Widespread precipitation arrives on Saturday as the low \nmoves further inland. Expect more fall-like temperatures as the \nweekend progresses. As far as precipitation goes, chances for \nwetting rain (more than 0.10 in) by Sunday will be about 70-80% \nfor west-central and southwest Montana, as well as Lemhi County. \nChances will be slightly better going east, and worse further west\nand north, with little to no precipitation for far northwest \nMontana. Temperatures rebound early next week, while ensemble \nsolutions are indicating more unsettled weather later in the week.",
